# kube-env for the V8 etcd FAIL-CLOSED scenarios. Rendered, sourced, never edited.
#
# AAP §0.5.1 (this file's CREATE row, "Jinja2 port of the Go text/template kube-env
# renderer") / AAP §0.4.1.1 layer L4 and AAP §0.4.2.1 (the test_etcd_failclosed.py
# blueprint, whose four scenarios are new automation with no Go ancestor) /
# AAP §0.10.2 (the etcd fail-closed boundary row: credentials absent with the
# opt-out not "true" must print the refusal AND exit 1, and PARTIAL credentials
# must ALSO exit 1) / tech-spec §2.1.8 (the function-local ":-true"
# backward-compatibility shim) / tech-spec §6.2.4.6 (etcd access-control
# hardening, control V8).
#
# INVARIANT LOCKED BY THIS FILE: every one of the fourteen assignments below is
# emitted UNCONDITIONALLY and independently, so all four fail-closed scenarios of
# cluster/gce/gci/configure-kubeapiserver.sh:configure-etcd-params are reachable
# by varying render-context VALUES alone - never by editing this template, never
# by coupling one credential to another, and never by defaulting a credential to
# something non-empty. An arbitrary subset of the six etcd mTLS credentials must
# stay expressible, because "a half-configured deployment must fail, not silently
# downgrade" is the property AAP §0.10.2 singles out as the subtle one.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL, GIVEN THREE TEMPLATES ARE ALREADY SHIPPED
#
# cluster/gce/gci/testdata/kube-apiserver/{base,etcd,kms}.template are read-only
# inputs, consumed as-is by the L1 shell modules and never duplicated into Python
# (AAP §0.4.4.2, "Referenced, not copied"). This file copies nothing from them and
# replaces none of them. It exists because ETCD_APISERVER_ALLOW_INSECURE - the
# one variable that selects between the plaintext-with-WARNING branch and the
# exit-1 branch - appears in NONE of the three: grep -l ALLOW_INSECURE over the
# trio returns no match. No combination of the shipped templates can express the
# fail-closed scenarios, and adding a field to a shipped template would edit an
# artifact this repository actually ships.
#
# It is also SELF-CONTAINED on purpose: it does not invoke the "base" template
# and does not restate its assignments. The trio declares its variables readonly,
# so a shell that sourced both a trio render and this one would abort on the
# duplicate assignment. Measured sufficient: KUBE_HOME plus the etcd variables
# drive every branch of configure-etcd-params, because the function reads nothing
# else.
#
# THE RENDER CONTRACT
#
# Target name: kube_env.j2 - Go's ParseFiles names each template after its base
# filename and this file defines no inner template, so its whole body IS the
# template. Dialect: scalar substitution only, the strict subset of the shipped
# trio's Go text/template syntax, so one renderer serves both. No statement
# blocks, no filters, no conditionals, no loops, no inheritance - and therefore
# no third-party template engine, which matters because jinja2 is not among the
# pinned test dependencies in python/requirements-test.txt.
#
# Context keys are the exported Go identifiers of kubeAPIServeETCDEnv
# (cluster/gce/gci/apiserver_etcd_test.go lines 26-43), which is exactly what
# tests/fixtures/etcd_env_cases.py publishes from
# KubeAPIServerETCDEnv.to_template_context() - thirteen of them are reused here
# verbatim, including the singular ETCDServersOverride spelling of the plural
# shell variable.
#
# ONE ADDITIONAL KEY, WHICH NO GO SOURCE CAN TELL YOU ABOUT: AllowInsecureEtcd.
# It has no counterpart in the Go struct and must not be given one - see the
# preservation note on TLS_FLAGS_CASES in tests/fixtures/etcd_env_cases.py, which
# keeps that dataclass a faithful sixteen-field mirror and delegates this
# variable here. The L4 context builder therefore supplies fourteen keys: the
# thirteen from to_template_context() that this file dereferences, plus
# AllowInsecureEtcd.
#
# THE TRAP: AN EMPTY AllowInsecureEtcd MEANS "true", WHICH MEANS PERMISSIVE
#
# configure-kubeapiserver.sh line 41 reads "${ETCD_APISERVER_ALLOW_INSECURE:-true}",
# and bash substitutes a :- default when the variable is unset OR NULL. Measured,
# with all six credentials empty: an empty value exits 0 down the WARNING branch,
# the variable being absent entirely exits 0 identically, and only the literal
# string false exits 1. So the fail-closed scenarios must render the literal
# false. Do NOT "fix" a failing scenario by blanking or deleting this assignment -
# that converts a fail-closed assertion into the permissive branch while the test
# still looks like it is testing something. Both GCE profiles default the variable
# to false (cluster/gce/config-default.sh:446, cluster/gce/config-test.sh:492,
# both documented there as a fail-closed safety net), which is why the
# credentials-absent-and-false scenario is the production-representative one and
# the credentials-absent-and-true scenario is the local/dev opt-in.
#
# EMPTY IS THE RIGHT IDIOM FOR THE OTHER THIRTEEN
#
# "Absent" is simply an empty rendered value: `readonly ETCD_APISERVER_CA_KEY=`
# is valid bash, passes bash -n, and satisfies the script's -z test. That is how
# the shipped etcd.template already drives TestTLSFlags "mTLS disabled" from a
# zero-value struct, so no conditional guard is needed here for any of them.
#
# THE THREE *_PATH VARIABLES ARE MANDATORY, NOT OPTIONAL
#
# configure-kubeapiserver.sh lines 23-25 dereference ETCD_APISERVER_CA_CERT_PATH,
# ETCD_APISERVER_CLIENT_CERT_PATH and ETCD_APISERVER_CLIENT_KEY_PATH with NO :-
# default, unlike every other read in the function. Measured: with all six
# credentials set but the paths undefined, the mTLS branch under set -u aborts
# with "line 23: ETCD_APISERVER_CA_CERT_PATH: unbound variable" and returns 127;
# with them defined the same invocation returns 0. Their values are paths, not
# key material, so obviously fake /foo/bar placeholders are sufficient - as they
# are in the shipped templates - and no real certificate, key or token belongs in
# any render context for this file.
#
# NOTES FOR THE CONSUMING TEST, NONE OF WHICH IS DISCOVERABLE FROM THIS FILE
#
#  1. configure-etcd-params takes a NAMEREF (line 19, local -n params_ref=$1), so
#     it must be invoked as: params="" ; configure-etcd-params params ; and then
#     "$params" read. A bare call with no argument does NOT fail loudly - measured,
#     it prints `local: not a valid identifier`, leaves the variable unset and
#     still exits 0. Consequently THIS TEMPLATE MUST NEVER DECLARE A VARIABLE
#     NAMED params: measured, adding `readonly params=` here makes the caller's
#     own assignment fail with "params: readonly variable" and exit 1 - a FALSE
#     rc==1 indistinguishable at a glance from a passing fail-closed assertion.
#  2. THE TWO STREAMS CARRY DIFFERENT THINGS, AND WHICH ONE IS PART OF THE
#     REQUIREMENT. The "refusing to fall back to plaintext etcd" ERROR (line 65)
#     and the partial-credential ERROR (line 70) are written with >&2 and arrive on
#     STDERR, which is what AAP §0.10.2, §0.4.2.1 and §0.5.2.1 each specify and what
#     this directory's own convention requires of a pre-exit diagnostic. The WARNING
#     (line 60) has no >&2 and arrives on STDOUT, alongside the parameter string the
#     caller echoes. Measured, with the streams captured separately:
#
#       all-absent + ALLOW_INSECURE=false   rc=1  stderr=refusal  stdout=EMPTY
#       partial    + ALLOW_INSECURE=false   rc=1  stderr=ERROR    stdout=EMPTY
#       all-absent + ALLOW_INSECURE=true    rc=0  stdout=WARNING  stderr=EMPTY
#       all six credentials present         rc=0  stdout=flags    stderr=EMPTY
#
#     So assert PER STREAM, using the separated-stream, tolerate-non-zero posture of
#     tests/helpers/bash.py (try_invoke_func, or run_bash for the nameref call
#     shape) with merge_streams=False. Never assert against the concatenation:
#     merged output is satisfied by EITHER stream, so it cannot distinguish a
#     correct refusal from one that regressed onto stdout - and on the fatal paths
#     stdout is exactly where a caller looks for the assembled flag string.
#  3. The exit 1 fires from INSIDE the function, so it terminates the whole bash
#     invocation: nothing sequenced after the call runs, and no params echo will
#     be seen for scenarios 2 and 3.
#  4. Sourcing configure-kubeapiserver.sh alone is sufficient for
#     configure-etcd-params - measured. configure-helper.sh is needed only by
#     start-kube-apiserver.
#  5. These assignments are readonly, and a sourced kube-env is read FIRST, so it
#     WINS over the process environment: measured, a scenario run with
#     ETCD_APISERVER_ALLOW_INSECURE=true exported still exits 1 when this file
#     renders false. Vary the render context, not the subprocess environment.
#  6. Storage options are deliberately absent. STORAGE_BACKEND,
#     STORAGE_MEDIA_TYPE and ETCD_COMPACTION_INTERVAL_SEC exist in the Go struct
#     and in etcd.template, but configure-etcd-params reads all three only through
#     ${VAR:-} guards (lines 66-75) so their absence is safe, and TestStorageOptions
#     already covers them at the L1 tier through the shipped template.
#  7. --etcd-servers-overrides is emitted only when ETCD_SERVERS is empty (the
#     default /events#http://127.0.0.1:4002 is appended) or when
#     ETCD_SERVERS_OVERRIDES is non-empty; with ETCD_SERVERS set and the override
#     empty it is absent entirely (lines 60-64, measured). ETCDServers and
#     ETCDServersOverride are therefore kept independently controllable.
#  8. EVERY SUBSTITUTED VALUE BELOW IS SHELL-QUOTED BY THE RENDERER, and the
#     assignments are written bare for exactly that reason. (This note cannot spell
#     an action out: a doubled brace anywhere in this file - comment or not - is
#     tokenised as one, because the renderer scans the whole text.)
#     The renderer's _format_value passes each substituted value through
#     tests.helpers.bash.shell_quote, which is
#     shlex.quote: this file is SOURCED by bash, so an unquoted value crosses from
#     data into code - a space splits the assignment and runs the remainder, and
#     $(...), a backtick or ${...} is EVALUATED rather than read. KUBE_HOME derives
#     from pytest's tmp_path and therefore from --basetemp, an externally supplied
#     argument, so that is not a hypothetical path. Do NOT add quotes here on top of
#     it: shlex.quote already returns ONE shell word, and wrapping a quoted word in
#     more quotes would embed the quote characters in the value. And do not remove
#     the quoting from the renderer to "match the shipped templates": shlex.quote is
#     a no-op for every value these cases carry - paths, https://127.0.0.1:2379,
#     base64, placeholder identifiers, `false` - so the rendered text is already
#     byte-identical to Go's text/template output for all of them.
#     python/tests/unit/shell/test_kube_env_quoting.py proves both halves by
#     rendering hostile values and having REAL bash source the result.
#
# There is no Apache-2.0 header here, and that is deliberate rather than an
# oversight: hack/boilerplate/boilerplate.py registers gated extensions by
# globbing boilerplate.*.txt, j2 is not among them, and get_files() collects a
# path only when its extension is registered - so this file is never gated. A
# header would satisfy no gate and would instead be rendered verbatim into the
# generated kube-env. Comments that reach the output are safe because they are
# shell comments; these lines are inert to bash and to bash -n.

# KUBE_HOME. Supplied per test from the caller's temporary directory, never a
# hardcoded absolute path, so cases stay isolated under pytest-xdist and
# pytest-randomly. configure-etcd-params does not read it; the surrounding
# harness and any full start-kube-apiserver invocation do.
readonly KUBE_HOME={{.KubeHome}}

# The six etcd mTLS credentials, and only these six, are what
# configure-etcd-params branches on (lines 21-58): all six non-empty selects
# mTLS, all six empty selects the plaintext-or-abort branch, and ANY other
# combination is the partial configuration that exits 1. Each is set
# independently so that arbitrary subset stays expressible. The shipped naming
# crosses two schemes and is reproduced as-is: the API server's own pair renders
# into the SERVER_ variables while the etcd pair renders into the CLIENT_ ones,
# because from etcd's point of view the API server is the client.
readonly ETCD_APISERVER_CA_KEY={{.CAKey}}
readonly ETCD_APISERVER_CA_CERT={{.CACert}}
readonly ETCD_APISERVER_SERVER_KEY={{.APIServerKey}}
readonly ETCD_APISERVER_SERVER_CERT={{.APIServerCert}}
readonly ETCD_APISERVER_CLIENT_KEY={{.ETCDKey}}
readonly ETCD_APISERVER_CLIENT_CERT={{.ETCDCert}}

# The etcd endpoint. Empty selects the script's own default per branch: https for
# mTLS (line 22), http for the plaintext fallback (line 42).
readonly ETCD_SERVERS={{.ETCDServers}}

# The three paths copied into --etcd-cafile, --etcd-certfile and --etcd-keyfile.
# Mandatory: see the *_PATH note above. Placeholders only.
readonly ETCD_APISERVER_CA_CERT_PATH={{.CACertPath}}
readonly ETCD_APISERVER_CLIENT_CERT_PATH={{.APIServerCertPath}}
readonly ETCD_APISERVER_CLIENT_KEY_PATH={{.APIServerKeyPath}}

# The events-store override. Shell variable plural, Go field singular; both
# spellings are as shipped.
readonly ETCD_SERVERS_OVERRIDES={{.ETCDServersOverride}}

# The uid the generated pod manifest runs as. Not read by configure-etcd-params,
# and supplied as the running uid rather than a literal, so a scenario that goes
# on to invoke start-kube-apiserver gets a value that exists on this machine.
readonly KUBE_API_SERVER_RUNASUSER={{.KubeAPIServerRunAsUser}}

# The fail-closed selector. MUST render the literal false for the fail-closed
# scenarios and the literal true for the local/dev opt-in scenario. An empty
# value means true - see THE TRAP above before changing anything on this line.
readonly ETCD_APISERVER_ALLOW_INSECURE={{.AllowInsecureEtcd}}
